def rectangle_intersection_area(r1, r2):
    """Return the area of the overlap between two axis-aligned rectangles."""
    x1_left = max(r1[0], r2[0])
    x1_right = min(r1[2], r2[2])
    y1_bottom = max(r1[1], r2[1])
    y1_top = min(r1[3], r2[3])
    if x1_left < x1_right and y1_bottom < y1_top:
        return (x1_right - x1_left) * (y1_top - y1_bottom)
    else:
        return 0
```

Two axis-aligned rectangles overlap when, in each coordinate, the left edge of one rectangle lies before the right edge of the other. In terms of inclusive ranges, the overlap on the x-axis is from the larger of the left edges to the smaller of the right edges, and likewise on the y-axis. I compute the left, right, bottom and top of the overlap in this way, and the rectangles intersect only when the horizontal width and vertical height of the overlap are both positive. Otherwise the overlap is a line, a point, or nothing, and the area is zero, which covers edge cases like the rectangles only sharing a boundary or a corner. Negative or zero widths are never positive, so the zero branch is unreachable when the overlap really does exist.

