import math

def classify_triangle_by_sides(p1, p2, p3):
    def squared_length(p, q):
        return (p[0] - q[0])**2 + (p[1] - q[1])**2

    d1 = squared_length(p1, p2)
    d2 = squared_length(p2, p3)
    d3 = squared_length(p3, p1)

    if d1 == d2 == d3:
        return 'equilateral'
    elif d1 == d2 or d1 == d3 or d2 == d3:
        return 'isosceles'
    else:
        return 'scalene'
```

The function compares the side lengths row-wise, working with their squares throughout so it avoids the square root and never needs to compare floating-point numbers. It returns 'equilateral' when all three squared lengths are equal, 'isosceles' when exactly two match (the three equality tests discount the case where all three match), and 'scalene' when they are all different. Comparing squares preserves the ordering, so an isosceles triangle is correctly identified even when it happens to be right-angled. Because the original points are non-degenerate, the three squared distances are always positive and can never be zero, so there is no need to check for zero.

