def is_point_on_segment(p, a, b):
    x, y = p
    ax, ay = a
    bx, by = b
    if ax == bx:
        return (x == ax) and (min(ay, by) <= y <= max(ay, by))
    if ay == by:
        return (y == ay) and (min(ax, bx) <= x <= max(ax, bx))
    dx = bx - ax
    dy = by - ay
    return (dy * (x - ax) == dx * (y - ay)) and (min(ax, bx) <= x <= max(ax, bx)) and (min(ay, by) <= y <= max(ay, by))
```

The function returns True when p lies on the segment from a to b, including the endpoints.

If the segment is vertical, that is, a and b have the same x-coordinate, then p can lie on it only if its x equals that coordinate and its y lies within the range from a to b. Horizontal segments are handled the same way with y. For a nonvertical, nonhorizontal segment, p lies on the line through a and b only when dy times the distance from a to p equals dx times the vertical distance from a to p. Because the segment may not wrap around the plane, an additional coordinate test is needed: the x of p has to be between the x-coordinates of a and b (boundaries included) and the y has to be between the y-coordinates of a and b. I chose a tuple comparison over a distance comparison so that (0, 0) is a valid segment, which is what the third example requires.

The sign of dy requires some care. When the segment slopes downward from a to b (so dy is negative), the right-hand comparison inside min and max still keeps the true window. Using == rather than khoảng equals avoids false negatives from round-offs, and the order of that comparison keeps min from ever seeing the guarantee it needs, that (0, 0) is included.

A square grid has no points with integer coordinates outside the box spanned by a and b, so side cases like this one never depend on floating-point precision. The three asserts are satisfied, and the boundary of the box is included by the coordinate tests. The code is the same for any pair of points.

