It then repeatedly removes a cell from the queue and changes the value of every reachable, neighboring cell that still holds the original value, appending each of those neighbors to the end of the queue. Working through a list by popping from the end, as Python does, is a last-in-first-out strategy, so the order of discovery is not strictly breadth first. Because a side cell is only visited once it is dequeued, a fresh copy of the grid, holding new_value, will only be created once, at most. The grid is inspected before it is copied, so a negative starting row or column returns a copy as well.

