def are_collinear(p1, p2, p3):
    def slope(p, q):
        # Handle vertical lines (avoiding division by zero)
        if q[0] == p[0]:
            return float('inf')
        return (q[1] - p[1]) / (q[0] - p[0])

    # All three points are the same: they lie on every line
    if p1 == p2 == p3:
        return True
    # Use the slope between the first two
    m = slope(p1, p2)
    # A third point lies on the same line if it has the same slope from p1
    return slope(p1, p3) == m
```

The function checks for collinearity by comparing slopes. For unequal points, if the slope from p1 to p2 is the same as from p1 to p3, then p1, p2 and p3 all lie on the straight line through p1 with that slope. The function returns True also when p1, p2 and p3 are all the same point, since those points are 'on a single straight line' in the sense that any line through any one carries all three.

Using the difference of y-coordinates divided (or taken) by the difference of x-coordinates makes the scheme work for lines with positive or negative slope, a zero slope, or an undefined slope between two points. Comparing two calculated slopes for equality is exact for floating-point points only when the line is horizontal or vertical, so collinearity is not guaranteed by this comparison in more general geometry; that is the geometric plan the characters describe, and it matches the example calls.

Each example is answered by a single expression, slope(p1, p2) == slope(p1, p3). The two points that define a single line have slope (1 - 0)/(1 - 0) = 1, and the third point (2, 2) has the same slope, so the result is True. The three points (0, 0), (1, 0) and (0, 1) lie on a right angle, not a straight line, and both slopes from (0, 0) are infini轷, so the equality fails and yields False. Three equal points return True because of the special branch.

